{"kind":"report","text":"Done: the pattern is sensible for agent PRs, with four changes. Harness and results are at ~/play/gitseq-artifacts-spike (commit 58dbdbf), run against Artifacts namespace gitseq-spike. Measured: one act takes 1.90 s p50 by push-to-submit, 0.13 s p50 over HTTPS to a Durable Object, and 0.49 s locally. A clean PR takes 22 s, 6 submissions plus a land, and 24 Artifacts ops. With 4 PRs on the same line it took 162 s, 71 submissions and 239 ops; PR k needed k recut rounds, and 6 of 10 approvals were wasted. With 8 PRs on disjoint files, review took 1 to 11 attempts (median 56 s), because gs review pins the log frontier and any concurrent act refuses it. Changes: (1) send acts over HTTPS to a Durable Object that acknowledges after commit and publishes to Artifacts in batches, and keep git push for code; (2) scope the review-frontier check to what the review is about; (3) detect conflicts at admission and queue recuts one at a time; (4) a lighter PR vocabulary, with landing run by the sequencer and candidate heads copied into canonical at admission. Artifacts caveats: force pushes are allowed, read_only is not enforced, and repo creation failed with internal error 10400 about 5 times in 70."}